./try
new and empty:
  pass a new queue is empty
  pass a new queue prints as nothing: ""
  pass head_get of empty is 0
  pass tail_get of empty is 0
  pass head_rem of empty is 0
  pass tail_rem of empty is 0
  (two warnings expected next:)
deq.c:65: warning: index 0 out of range [0,0)
  pass head_ith of empty is 0, with a warning
deq.c:65: warning: index 0 out of range [0,0)
  pass tail_ith of empty is 0, with a warning
put:
  pass head_put onto empty: "b"
  pass head_put prepends: "a b"
  pass tail_put appends: "a b c"
  pass tail_put onto empty: "b"
  pass puts at both ends interleave: "a b c"
  pass head_put reverses: "4 3 2 1"
  pass tail_put preserves: "1 2 3 4"
ith:
  pass head_ith(0) is the head
  pass head_ith counts from the head
  pass head_ith(len-1) is the tail
  pass tail_ith(0) is the tail
  pass tail_ith counts from the tail
  pass tail_ith(len-1) is the head
  (four warnings expected next:)
deq.c:65: warning: index 5 out of range [0,5)
  pass head_ith past the end is 0
deq.c:65: warning: index -1 out of range [0,5)
  pass head_ith of a negative index is 0
deq.c:65: warning: index 5 out of range [0,5)
  pass tail_ith past the end is 0
deq.c:65: warning: index -1 out of range [0,5)
  pass tail_ith of a negative index is 0
  pass ith leaves the queue alone: "a b c d e"
get:
  pass head_get returns the head
  pass head_get removes the head: "b c d e"
  pass tail_get returns the tail
  pass tail_get removes the tail: "b c d"
  pass one datum left: "d"
  pass the last datum comes out either end
  pass the queue is empty again: ""
  pass head_get of a drained queue is 0
  pass a drained queue can be refilled: "z"
rem:
  pass head_rem returns what it found
  pass head_rem removes from the middle: "a b d e"
  pass head_rem finds the head
  pass tail_rem finds the tail
  pass both ends can be remmed away: "b d"
  pass head_rem of an absent datum is 0
  pass tail_rem of an absent datum is 0
  pass a failed rem changes nothing: "b d"
  pass rem compares by ==, not by value
  pass head_rem takes the occurrence nearest the head
  pass tail_rem takes the occurrence nearest the tail
map and str:
  pass map visits every datum, head to tail
  pass str applies its function
  pass map and str leave the queue alone: "a b c d e"
  pass map of an empty queue does nothing
  pass str of an empty queue is empty
del:
  pass a queue of heap data: "n0 n1 n2"
  pass del maps over every datum before freeing
  pass del of an emptied queue is clean

